---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Window from "../../components/Window.astro";
import banner from "../../assets/img/ceojoe-banner.webp";
import showcase from "../../assets/img/ceojoe-showcase.webp";
---

<Base
  title="CeoJoe — case study — Ayach Youssef"
  description="CeoJoe: the site for my small web studio, with a showcase of sixty live demo sites. Three hero redesigns, an image pipeline driven through a browser, and a rule that demos are never presented as client work."
>
  <section class="study-head container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Case study — my own studio, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="display">CeoJoe</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The site for CeoJoe, the small studio I take client work through. Its job
      is to show, not claim: every product on it can be opened, and its showcase
      links to live demo sites built with
      <a href="/projects/site-kit/">Site Kit</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="meta-band">
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Role</span>
        <p>Direction and review; built with AI coding agents</p>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Timeline</span>
        <p>October — November 2026</p>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Stack</span>
        <p>Astro &middot; GSAP &middot; one React island &middot; Vercel</p>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Status</span>
        <p>
          Live at <a
            href="https://ceojoe.vercel.app"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">ceojoe.vercel.app</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2 class="display">A new studio has no client list to show</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          A studio site usually leans on logos and testimonials. A studio that
          is a few months old has neither, and inventing them is the fastest way
          to lose the one thing it does have. So the rule for this site was set
          before any design: nothing on it may imply a client that does not
          exist. Products are labelled as our own. Demo sites are labelled as
          concept demos. Team cards for roles nobody holds yet say
          &ldquo;we&rsquo;re hiring&rdquo; instead of showing a stock face.
        </p>
        <p>
          What a new studio can show is range and finish. That became the brief:
          one site per kind of business, each complete, each live, each in
          French and Arabic.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal" style="margin-top: var(--space-12)">
        <Window
          title="ceojoe — home banner"
          src={banner}
          alt="The CeoJoe home page banner: the headline 'A site that fills your tables', labelled '01 / 08 Hotels and Restaurants', beside a browser frame and a phone frame showing the live Riad des Lanternes demo site in French"
          caption="The banner plays one real demo per field, in that demo's own colours, then rests. Pause, replay, arrow keys; no autoplay under reduced motion."
          eager
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">What I would point at</p>
        <h2 class="display">Four decisions</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="bento">
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 0">
          <h3>The hero took several tries</h3>
          <p>
            First a Three.js scene: impressive, heavy, and it sat behind the
            text. Then a typewriter build log: lighter, but about the site
            instead of the customer. Then a portrait. The current one is real
            screenshots of real demos. The Three.js dependency is gone entirely.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 1">
          <h3>A photo per demo, no API bill</h3>
          <p>
            Each demo needed its own photograph. Generation ran through a
            consumer image tool driven in the browser, one prompt per trade,
            with a shared style prefix so the images read as one set. One batch
            landed shifted by a file; a contact sheet caught it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 2">
          <h3>A build that refuses a typo</h3>
          <p>
            The showcase groups demos into eight categories from one manifest. A
            site with a category that is not in the list fails the build, where
            it used to vanish from the page without a word. Thumbnails are
            served at two densities from the site&rsquo;s own assets.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 3">
          <h3>Accessibility found in dark mode</h3>
          <p>
            A computed-contrast scan over every page in both themes found
            failures a visual check had missed, all the same mistake: a text
            colour that flipped with the theme on a background that did not. One
            was the skip link, invisible until keyboard focus.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal" style="margin-top: var(--space-12)">
        <Window
          title="ceojoe — showcase"
          src={showcase}
          alt="The CeoJoe showcase page: the Food and Hospitality category with six demo cards, each a photograph, a business name such as Le Grain Chaud or La Mie Dorée, and a 'View live demo' link"
          caption="Fifty-six demos, grouped by trade. Every card opens a complete live site."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Honest limits</p>
        <h2 class="display">What this is not</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          It is not proof of client results; there are none on it, by design.
          The site and the demos were built with AI coding agents under my
          direction and review. It still runs on a Vercel subdomain while the
          real domain is chosen, and it has had automated accessibility checks
          but not a full screen-reader pass.
          <a href="/log/sixty-sites-one-afternoon-of-images/"
            >The Ship Log entry</a
          > covers the image pipeline and what went wrong in it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="featured-actions reveal" style="margin-top: var(--space-12)">
        <a
          href="https://ceojoe.vercel.app"
          class="btn btn-primary"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Open CeoJoe</a
        >
        <a
          href="https://ceojoe.vercel.app/showcase"
          class="btn"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Browse the showcase</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
